To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you need to convolve your gray-scale image with a Gaussian kernel of appropriate size (usually larger than the Sobel kernel) and then apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image. Here's the modified code for implementing this:

```MATLAB
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Define Gaussian kernel parameters
sigma = 3; % Standard deviation
kernel_size = 2*round(sigma*sqrt(2))+1; % Size of Gaussian kernel

% Generate Gaussian kernel
[gaussian_kernel, gaussian_kernel_center] = fspecial('gaussian', [kernel_size kernel_size], sigma);

% Filter input image using Gaussian kernel
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'replicate');

% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator to filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions and normalize intensity
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
edge_image = (edge_image - min(edge_image(:))) / (max(edge_image(:)) - min(edge_image(:)));

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

