To make the circle element in your CSS code look more 3D, you can apply some additional CSS properties such as perspective, transform, and box-shadow. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` selector with these properties added:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  transform: translateZ(10px);
}

/* Add this rule for better 3D effect */
.circle:hover {
  transform: translateX(-5px) translateY(-5px) rotateX(5deg) rotateY(5deg) scale(1.1);
}

/* To create depth of field */
.background {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  z-index: -1;
  backface-visibility: hidden;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
}

/* Set up the background image */
.background img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}
```

The `transform-style: preserve-3d` property is used to enable 3D transformation on all child elements of the container. The `translateZ()` property adds depth to the circle by moving it slightly forward along the z-axis. You can adjust the value of `translateZ()` to control how far away or close the circle appears.

Additionally, you can add a hover effect to the circle to give it a more dynamic feel. In this example, when the user hovers over the circle, we apply a combination of translations, rotations, and scaling to give it a 3D rotation effect.

