To improve efficiency for handling large amounts of text, here are some optimizations you can make:

1. Use a memory-mapped file instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This will allow you to access the text as if it were loaded into memory without actually loading it all at once. Here's how you can modify the code above to use memory-mapping:

```matlab
% Open the files with memory mapping
fh1 = mmap('r', 'Text1.txt');
fh2 = mmap('r', 'Text2.txt');

% Seek to the beginning of each file
seek(fh1, 0, 'bof');
seek(fh2, 0, 'bof');

% Convert text to lowercase and split into words while reading from the file
words1 = lower(strsplit(fread(fh1, inf, 'char')))';
words2 = lower(strsplit(fread(fh2, inf, 'char')))';

% Close the file handles and free the memory maps
close(fh1);
close(fh2);
unmap(fh1);
unmap(fh2);

% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

2. You can also consider using vectorized operations instead of loops, which can be much faster than iterating over arrays element by element. Here's an example of how you could vectorize the `setdiff()` operation:

```matlab
% Combine the two sets of words into a single array
combinedWords = [words1; words2];

% Remove duplicates and sort the unique words
uniqueWords = sort(unique(combinedWords));

% Extract the subset of unique words that only appear in text1
text1WordsOnly = uniqueWords(~ismember(uniqueWords, words2));

% Display the text1-specific words
disp(text1WordsOnly);
```

