Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
Here's a C++ implementation of the bubble sort algorithm for sorting the given array:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>

namespace json = nlohmann;

// Bubble Sort Algorithm
void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    bool swapped = true;
    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false;
        for (int i=0; i<n-1; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
                std::swap(arr[i], arr[i+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        n--; // reduce size of array after each iteration
    }
}

// Read JSON input and extract integer array
json::json readJSONInput() {
    std::string jsonStr;
    std::cout << "Enter JSON input:\n";
    std::getline(std::cin, jsonStr);
    return json::parse(jsonStr);
}

// Write sorted integer array to JSON output
void writeJSONOutput(const std::vector<int>& arr) {
    json::json j = {{ "sortedArray", arr }};
    std::string jsonStr = j.dump();
    std::cout << "Sorted Array:\n" << jsonStr;
}

// Main Function
int main() {
    try {
        // Read JSON Input
        auto j = readJSONInput();
        // Extract Integer Array
        auto arr = j["array"];
        // Sort Array Using Bubble Sort
        bubbleSort(arr);
        // Write Sorted Array to Output
        writeJSONOutput(arr.get<std::vector<int>>());
    } catch (...) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Invalid JSON input.\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first include the necessary libraries for handling JSON input and output using `nlohmann/json`. We define our own namespace `json` to avoid conflicts with other libraries that might use `json` as well.

